Package: dtasamplesize
Type: Package
Title: Exact Bayesian Assurance Sample Sizes for Diagnostic Accuracy
Version: 0.7.0
Authors@R:
    person("Jes\u00fas D.", "Rojas", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           email = "jrojasrivero@gmail.com",
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0003-0912-490X"))
Description: Provides sample size methods for diagnostic test accuracy (DTA)
    studies: exact calculations where possible and Monte Carlo
    simulation otherwise. Implements Bayesian assurance (Wilson et al. 2022
    <doi:10.1002/sim.9393>), computed exactly by Beta-Binomial enumeration
    (no simulation) or, optionally, by Monte Carlo, with separate design and
    analysis priors, power priors from pilot data, an exact assurance curve
    and prior sensitivity analyses; joint sample size for sensitivity and
    specificity by Monte Carlo, with a deterministic AUC gate; adjustment for
    imperfect reference
    standards via an exactly-identified misclassification correction (with
    a closed form for the apparent sensitivity/specificity and a delta-
    method variance for the bias-corrected sensitivity/specificity; see also
    Staquet et al. 1981 <doi:10.1016/0021-9681(81)90059-X> and, for the
    retained prevalence variance-inflation diagnostic, Rogan & Gladen 1978
    <doi:10.1093/oxfordjournals.aje.a112510>),
    adaptive prevalence re-estimation (Stark & Zapf 2020
    <doi:10.1177/0962280220913588>), sample size for time-dependent ROC
    curves, and sample size for net benefit (decision curve analysis).
    Addresses limitations of classical formulas (Buderer 1996
    <doi:10.1111/j.1553-2712.1996.tb03538.x>), whose achieved precision is
    evaluated by exact binomial enumeration or by Monte Carlo simulation.
    Includes a unified framework that integrates all approaches.
    The validated scope is the bam_*() functions (bam_sample_size(method =
    "exact"), bam_assurance_curve(), bam_sensitivity(),
    bam_sensitivity_local(), bam_prior_contour(), bam_prior_from_pilot() and
    bam_prior_data_conflict()), joint_sample_size() and
    mc_validate_buderer(method = "exact"); ss_unified(), ss_net_benefit(),
    ss_imperfect_ref(), ss_adaptive_prevalence() and ss_time_dependent_roc()
    are experimental and outside that validated scope.
